{
 "ep": 21,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "pg_hba.conf: Client Authentication",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "One pg_hba.conf line can block your application.",
   "cap": "One *pg_hba.conf* line can *block your app.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode twenty one of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: who may connect, and safe changes.",
   "cap": "Episode 21: who may *connect*, and *safe changes.*",
   "cue": "safe"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_hba.conf is the client authentication file. HBA means host based.",
   "cap": "*HBA* = *host-based authentication.*",
   "cue": "authentication"
  },
  {
   "say": "By default it lives in the data directory. SHOW hba_file prints the real path.",
   "cap": "*SHOW hba_file* prints the real path.",
   "cue": "path"
  },
  {
   "say": "On Ubuntu packages, it is under slash etc instead.",
   "cap": "Ubuntu packages: under */etc.*",
   "cue": "etc"
  },
  {
   "say": "Each line is one rule: connection type, database, user, client address and method.",
   "cap": "One line = *type, database, user, address, method.*",
   "cue": "method"
  },
  {
   "say": "Type local is a Unix socket. Type host is a network connection, with or without SSL.",
   "cap": "*local* = Unix socket. *host* = TCP/IP.",
   "cue": "SSL"
  },
  {
   "say": "hostssl needs SSL. hostnossl matches only connections without it.",
   "cap": "*hostssl* = SSL only. *hostnossl* = no SSL.",
   "cue": "hostnossl"
  },
  {
   "say": "Addresses use CIDR. Slash thirty two is one IPv4 host. All zeros, slash zero, is every IPv4 address.",
   "cap": "*/32* = one host. *0.0.0.0/0* = every IPv4 address.",
   "cue": "every"
  },
  {
   "say": "Methods: scram-sha-256 checks a password, and peer checks the local OS user.",
   "cap": "*scram-sha-256* = password. *peer* = OS user.",
   "cue": "peer"
  },
  {
   "say": "reject refuses the connection, and trust lets anyone in with no password.",
   "cap": "*reject* refuses. *trust* = *no password.*",
   "cue": "trust"
  },
  {
   "say": "The rule people miss: lines are read top to bottom, and the first match is used.",
   "cap": "*Top to bottom.* The *first match* is used.",
   "cue": "first"
  },
  {
   "say": "No fall through. If that line's authentication fails, later lines are never tried.",
   "cap": "*No fall-through:* later lines are never tried.",
   "cue": "never"
  },
  {
   "say": "We tested it. Line two asks app_user for a SCRAM password. Line three says trust.",
   "cap": "Line 2: *scram-sha-256.* Line 3: *trust.*",
   "cue": "trust"
  },
  {
   "say": "A wrong password still failed. The trust line was never reached. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "Wrong password: *failed.* Trust line *ignored.*",
   "cue": "never"
  },
  {
   "say": "A reject line above the normal rule gives: pg_hba.conf rejects connection.",
   "cap": "A *reject* line on top blocks the app.",
   "cue": "rejects"
  },
  {
   "say": "With no matching line at all: no pg_hba.conf entry for host, user, database.",
   "cap": "*no pg_hba.conf entry* = no line matched.",
   "cue": "entry"
  },
  {
   "say": "Saving is not enough. Reload with pg_ctl reload, pg_reload_conf, or a SIGHUP.",
   "cap": "Changes apply only after a *reload.*",
   "cue": "SIGHUP"
  },
  {
   "say": "Check first. The view pg_hba_file_rules has an error column for broken lines.",
   "cap": "Check first: *pg_hba_file_rules.*",
   "cue": "error"
  },
  {
   "say": "Our typo, a missing dash in scram-sha-256, was flagged: invalid authentication method.",
   "cap": "Typo caught: *invalid authentication method.*",
   "cue": "invalid"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_reload_conf still returned true, but the log said: not reloaded.",
   "cap": "Returned *true*, but *was not reloaded.*",
   "cue": "log"
  },
  {
   "say": "So the old rules stayed. Always read the log after a reload.",
   "cap": "Old rules *stay.* Read the *log.*",
   "cue": "log"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: trust from any address. Anyone who reaches the server logs in as any user.",
   "cap": "*trust* from anywhere = *no password at all.*",
   "cue": "Anyone"
  },
  {
   "say": "A common practice: tight rules first, scram-sha-256, and only the networks you need.",
   "cap": "Tight rules first. *scram-sha-256.* Real networks.",
   "cue": "networks"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: first match wins, no fall through. Check the view, reload, then read the log.",
   "cap": "Recap: *first match wins.* Check, reload, read the log.",
   "cue": "log"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: password authentication and SCRAM. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *passwords and SCRAM.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
